dicted by  evidence and things like this but in in  researching this book again i've covered  the pentagon for many years i've been a  foreign correspondent before that so i'm  used to spin and people giving happy  talk uh and i get that i'm not saying  that that's a lie but there's certainly  a lot of that a lot of misleading  statements but when you look at the  actual documents  and  the correlation in time between what  somebody said in public and what they  clearly knew at the time was true or  false i mean you can't do anything but  conclude they were lying like with  rumsfeld saying  major combat operations are over in in  2003. i mean there's no doubt he knew  that wasn't true at the time and you  you've got contemporaneous evidence and  testimony by people who served under him  who said this just flat out wasn't true  and just like obama saying it was the  end of  the combat mission he knew that wasn't  true he was the commander-in-chief and  he had authorized these troops to keep  fighting in combat  so those are two big  big lies right there's no way around it  i mean how else can you describe it at  the same time you're right it wasn't  throughout the world there also was a  lot of  uh  you know inflation of expectations a lot  of uh people spinning things a lot of  rosy characterizations of what's going  on that you know doesn't quite cross the  line into lying but you know add it all  up and it's just it's not a good record  of anybody telling the truth as i  mentioned the one general who told the  truth got fired and i think the lesson  out from that was  you can't be honest with the american  people and  you know the old cliche is you know  truth is the first casualty of war so  there's a long history of this  but at the same time when is there a  more important moment for our us  government leaders to be honest with the  public when people's lives are on the  line and we're committing troops and a  lot of money  to a conflict halfway around the world i  mean they need to start being honest  about it  in his speech  this week president biden stated  categorically that the war in  afghanistan is over  um at the same time  he said we will maintain the fight  against terrorism in afghanistan um  and we just don't need to fight a ground  war to do it we can strike you know from  afar  um with american boots on the ground  very few if needed  right um  there seemed to be some wiggle room  there you know and  based on what you've  seen over the past two decades you know  what do you imagine future  us military action in afghanistan if any  could could look like  well that's you know another phrase that  people should watch out for is boots on  the ground that's something that obama  said a lot and people may forget this  but when he sent troops to back to iraq  and syria  at first he you know said particularly  in syria there will be no boots on the  ground we're not putting u.s troops on  the ground in syria and of course they  did right and reporters would say this  what do you mean no boots on the ground  there's we've got pictures of literally  of boots on the ground of americans and  they would use this to kind of again  give people this misleading image that  we don't have lots of troops there uh  you know i i do think biden  i take him at his word for the moment  that there is this complete troop  withdrawal from afghanistan and we're  not going to be you know there's no  afghan army or government to advise  anymore right so  that role's gone that's vanished there's  no purpose for having them there uh  we're not going to be working  hand-in-glove with the taliban that same  way but does that mean that the cia  won't send operatives in i i doubt i  think that you know the cia will try and  find some way to have a presence they'll  have proxy forces perhaps uh you know  the war is going to continue in a  different phase it's just not going to  be this overt  military conflict but you know it could  be more like um you know you think in  the afghan war when the soviets were in  there we were involved in that war even  though we didn't technically have boots  on the ground but we were a big player  in the war between the soviets and the  afghan resistance at that point i'm not  saying that's what's going to happen now  but i'm just saying you're right there's  a lot of wiggle room there for the u.s  to remain  involved in afghanistan for  counter-terrorism operations  yeah there were there were suitcases of  money on the ground um back in back in  the 80s um  so  a few questions um from  from the  the audience um  one um let's see  uh gabriel asks um you know craig thank  you for your reporting since the pull  out from afghanistan there's been some  conversation around how u.s military  contractors have benefited from the the  prolonged war  did you come across this topic in the  course of your reporting um if so what  what did it look like i mean the the  role of contractors in in war is also a  great way to kind of fudge boots on the  ground um so what what did you what did  you uh make of that in the course of  your reporting whether for this book or  just your your overall reporting on on  the you know the global war on terror  well exactly and that's a good way to  be skeptical of what's going on but  certainly uh military contractors uh you  know they earned a lot of money  throughout the war in fact  just about everybody did in afghanistan  who had any kind of presence there the  irony is we spent so much money on  defense contracts and supplying our  troops whether it was ammunition or  water or food or  you know we had to send this enormous  amount of supplies halfway around the  world to this landlocked country in  afghanistan i mean i think people  sometimes don't realize what a  logistical challenge and feat that was  but we also spent enormous amounts of  money doing it and people  whether they're u.s defense contractors  foreign defense contractors uh afghans  uh even the taliban they all benefited  from this i mean one thing we would see  in the interviews for the afghanistan  papers was just how much money was being  siphoned off  uh not just by  uh  you know crooks in afghanistan you know  corrupt contractors but the taliban the  contractors had to pay protection money  to the taliban to let their convoys go  through and during obama's term during  the surge the military essentially  conducted a sort of review of all the  defense contracts uh at that point there  were more than 10 billion dollars a year  being spent in afghanistan and they  concluded that as much as 40 of the  money was being siphoned off and it  either went to uh criminal networks or  the taliban or corrupt afghan officials  so  everybody was benefiting from this uh  you know the good guys and the bad guys  and you know that certainly reduces any  incentive uh to end the fighting no  question  uh that gets uh perfectly to a question  that shmuel asks would things have ended  differently had we eradicated corruption  in afghanistan i guess one way to think  about that had we not  aggravated it  as well  well i think that's maybe the better way  to look at it and it's  uh  one state department official barnett  rubin said in an interview a lessons  learned learned interview he said you  know there's one necessary ingredient  for corruption and that's money and we  were the ones with all the money right  and because we were flooding afghanistan  with more money than it could possibly  absorb whether it was defense contracts  or nation building programs or  humanitarian aid uh inevitably a lot of  this money ended up in people's pockets  who who shouldn't have gotten it so  on one hand the united states was  lecturing the afghan government you know  you need to stamp out corruption and we  would train judges and set up courts to  deal with this uh on the other hand uh  we're making it happen because we're  we're sending all this money over there  you know there's there's one  episode in particular i was struck by  there was a diplomatic cable around 2006  that was sent by the us ambassador in  afghanistan at the time his name was ron  newman and he talked about meeting with  karzai hamid karzai the afghan president  in which uh the ambassador was berating  karzai about all these corrupt officials  on his payroll  uh and the afghan government and he was  saying that  in particular he was singling out  karzai's half-brother  ahmed wali karzai who was kind of a  strong man in kandahar in southern  afghanistan saying you need to remove  him from power he's corrupt to the core  and karzai of course was furious about  this because in part this is his brother  but his comeback was  you're the guys who are giving him all  the money that he's on the cia's payroll  and your the defense department's giving  them all these contracts why are you why  are you blaming me you're the guys who  are you know he's your best friend down  there and of course what can you say to  that right on one hand we lecture the  afghans you need to clean things up  but we were responsible for a lot of  those problems now the question is if we  had eradicated corruption would have  things been successful i i think it was  it was too difficult to eradicate  corruption both because of our role in  it and because the afghans saw this as a  way they didn't know when the money was  going to go away and they're all trying  to get a piece of it because they don't  know how the war is going to turn out i  think it would have been very hard  um  but the most devastating part of the  corruption was that it really made the  afghan people lose faith in their own  government they saw their own leaders as  a bunch of thieves who were looking out  for their own interests and so in the  end particularly in the rural areas uh  afghans sided with the taliban over  their own government and you know there  was no way we were going to win the war  if that was going to happen  uh getting back to this notion of  progress that we've we've discussed a  little bit uh bonnie asks you know  people point to improved conditions for  women and girls in afghanistan  in as progress  um is that  valid  sure absolutely um you know the question  is what is that progress going to  be sustainable or not and right now  that's a very open question a real  concern  um and this was but this is a really  good question because this gets to the  point of what we're trying what were we  trying to accomplish in afghanistan when  we went to war in 2001 it was to go  after al qaeda it wasn't to  uh  improve the lot of women and girls in  afghanistan that's certainly a noble  goal no question but that isn't why we  went to war but that became part of the  mission creep as it were  the bush administration  rightfully so said we should stick up we  should try and improve human rights in  afghanistan while we're there but this  became mixed up with the original  purpose of the war and i think a lot of  times the americans  you know we tend to we tend to project  our own values on another country and to  what degree can you really do that and  hope it will take root i don't know  kabul is a very different place from the  rest of afghanistan and that in the  urban areas in afghanistan like kabul or  herat or mazarish reef uh you know there  is uh  the the  the lot of women and girls has come a  long way you know they they're able to  go to school they don't have they can  take jobs they have important positions  in the government and you know i don't  want to minimize that  uh but in rural areas uh i i think we  were overreaching a bit for imposing our  own values and what afghans were ready  for and that has to be something that  comes from them in part and so this was  something we we wrestled with but again  never really sorted out what were we  hoping to accomplish what was our  purpose for being there and while there  was progress made in many regards  the whole question is now that we lost  the war what's going to happen  speaking of what's going to happen  let's get a little bit into into what  has just happened  president biden has expressed in recent  days that  uh look it was impossible to kind of get  out without some degree of chaos this  was always going to be the case and  and karina asks if this is true was  there would there have been any better  way the vitamin creation could have  avoided this chaotic exit from  afghanistan  well i think they could have planned a  lot better no question uh you know just  they clearly were uh caught off guard by  the degree by how quickly the taliban  was able to  take over provincial capitals and really  just march right into kabul without any  resistance they clearly  were not ready for that i think  intelligence assessments show that the  binding administration thought  they had at least a few months if not  longer that the afghan government and  army could hold out so they weren't  prepared for this and they should have  uh  they should have been they should have  had contingencies in place that you know  the us military plans for everything you  know they're at the pentagon there's  plans on the shelf for invading canada  nobody ever expects to use them but  there's plans and i am surprised that we  didn't have better plans in place for a  sudden evacuation if we needed it and  particularly the  planning was uh  was deficient in trying to process  immigrant visas for afghans who would  help the united states as interpreters  or civil servants or other roles like  that  the us government knew this dating back  to the obama administration that there  was this huge backlog of visa  applications that hadn't been processed  and couldn't be done quickly i think the  binding administration had made some  improvements in that in the margin since  january but uh that was something that  we should have foreseen and should have  been better prepared for uh so in the  end we had to do this big evacuation but  uh you know we were just we there was no  way we could process all those  applications we were just pulling people  out willy-nilly and it was just very  very chaotic so  yes they could have done better at the  same time you know there's never any  easy way to end a war that you're losing  it's easy to start one but it's a lot  harder to end it  and you look at the last seven years  obama and trump and biden all tried to  end the war obama promised he would by  the end of his second term and the  reason he didn't is he was worried  there'd be a collapse of the afghan  government so we kept troops over there  same thing with trump he promised to end  the war but his generals advised him  said look if you pull out now the afghan  government could collapse uh so you need  to keep some troops here even though you  cut this deal with the taliban to  withdraw he kind of kicked it down the  road for biden to deal with so yeah  biden made the decision to finally end  the war and it probably was going to be  chaotic no matter what but it didn't  have to be that chaotic  uh  i have time for for a couple of more  questions um i'm gonna focus in on on  two that have to do uh with  with journalism um both you know in a  micro sense and in a macro sense i'll  i'll take the the micro sense first  linda asks  do you have researchers or other staff  that work with you or was this a solo  operation  did you work on other assignments over  the course of your work on the  afghanistan papers  uh no i didn't work on other assignments  this this was my full-time job when we  were publishing the series in the post  and then for  uh  you know in 2020 and so far in 2021 this  is all i did was work on the book and i  did my own research for it but i  certainly had an enormous amount of  assistance in the newsroom uh on the  original series in terms of uh  you know editors  fact checkers uh page designers we we  posted all these documents on the  internet and made them easy to search so  people could see them so  we had this huge effort on the staff  photographers videographers designers  so many people helped with this  i was the the sole reporter on it on the  book again i was the author but had  tremendous help in the newsroom in terms  of editing and presentation and uh i did  have a fact checker full-time who could  who worked on fact checking for the book  that isn't something we ordinarily  usually reporters responsible for his or  her own fact checking for news stories  uh so if this is a washington post post  book and it drawn the talents of people  across the newsroom no question  i should point out as a as a fellow post  staffer that on projects like like these  it's not uncommon to see  um you know multiple  reporters and multiple bylines um and  one of the things that was so amazing  when afghanistan papers first came out  as a post project is that it was like  by craig whitlock you know and  it was um it was it was uh an  arduous um  and and extraordinary uh work but as  you're right there's there's a big team  um the other question about journalism  that um  is is more of a more of a macro question  um  comes from here let me scroll down to it  um  is uh robert robert asks did the press  fail in its role  to inform the american public  about the failures you're reporting now  yeah so i think the press always can do  a better job uh that said i maybe i  don't want to appear defensive but i  think  many reporters did a very good job of  covering the war in afghanistan and many  of these issues were reported were made  public about uh corruption in  afghanistan about uh about the problems  in training the afghan army and police  force uh you know certainly lots of  stories questioning uh whether this  withdrawal was going to go well and  negotiations with the taliban and you  know there was a lot of questioning  skeptical coverage through the years  but maybe you know we didn't do enough  of it or maybe certain outlets didn't do  more of it there's no question in recent  years since 2014 when obama  you know announced an end to the combat  mission a lot of reporters left kabul uh  and the television networks in  particular haven't given as much  attention over there and  afghanistan kind of was became the  forgotten war for a while so did we need  to cover it more closely absolutely i  mean we look at what happened and  there's no question we could have done  more but i also don't want to minimize  the risks and sacrifices that many  journalists made in going to afghanistan  and staying there the washington post  has had a bureau ever in kabul ever  since 2001 uh so of other newspapers uh  the wire services like the associated  press and reuters have covered things  there diligently and a lot of people  have put themselves at real risk to do  so so i i don't want to minimize that  but could we have done a better job you  know sure absolutely  but i don't know that we failed  we have i'm going to sneak in one last  um question i i was struck from the very  beginning that  uh both your your reporting in the post  on the afghanistan papers and the book  um you know has its genesis in in this  lessons learned project and that that  impulse is wonderful right to that that  military officials um and diplomatic  officials would  would want to um  you know get that down to to sort of  like assess what what they have learned  um  what do you think we have learned what  are the lessons learned from  the afghan war  i don't know that we've learned them uh  i think maybe we've learned that we  don't want to you know get in over our  head and take on these  enormous ambitious  uh  security projects to remake countries  overseas  particularly countries we don't  understand very well i think maybe  there's some humility in order here  uh and i think certainly in public  opinion nobody's clamoring uh to go  invade another country and try and  transform the society so i i think  there's a recognition that we  overreached in afghanistan for a long  time  um  that said i mean you know vietnam  happened and we thought we maybe we  learned our lessons from there and we  didn't and someday there's going to be  another crisis somewhere in the world  and the united states uh tends to react  militarily if it's under threat and the  question is how are we going to to react  and how are we going to deal with a  situation that may come up that may be  similar but in some other country and  some other part of the world and  you know i'm i'm skeptical that we've  learned our lessons the lessons may be  there  to read and see and hear  but are they going to sink in i guess  that's the question and only time will  tell with that  well craig i can't imagine  a more timely moment for your work to  come out uh in in this book um and i  i hope that it'll help us  um  reflect and and and maybe even learn  some of those those lessons  well thank you carlos thank you for the  the great discussion i really want to  thank the striker center for hosting us  it was a real privilege and honor uh  these are terrific questions and  it is encouraging  uh the public response people do want to  know what went wrong in afghanistan and  they want to hear it straight and they  want an explanation of what happened  over the last 20 years how things could  go wrong seemingly so quickly and i i  hope the book has those answers and will  give people a much better feel  for for why things went wrong  on behalf of the stryker center craig  and carlos we want to thank you for  joining us this evening and thank you  all of you for tuning in  and uh please check our website for many  great fall programming and wishing  everyone celebrating a very happy rosh  hashanah thank you  and now on book tv we're live with  former vanderbilt university professor  and vice chair of
 

